#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-run-workflow: the run: blocks of the steps of a workflow file, run
here as GitHub runs them, on the one system this is started on.

Usage
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow [WORKFLOW]          every job and step of the
                                                     file, by default
                                                     .github/workflows/stock-take.yml,
                                                     each run: block with the
                                                     shell the file gives it
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow --list [WORKFLOW]   the plan alone: every step
                                                     with the command its shell
                                                     makes of it and whether it
                                                     runs here; nothing is run
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow --self-test         every outcome once, in a
                                                     throwaway directory outside
                                                     the repository

Exit 0 where every step that runs here ended with 0 and no step was left
unrun but by design; 1 where a step ended otherwise than 0, was stopped at the
job's timeout, ended the process of this program that holds it, or could not
be run here; 2 where the file cannot be read as a workflow or the repository
cannot be found. The last line says which: it opens with "green:" or with
"red:". Ended itself by SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP, on macOS, Linux and
Alpine, it ends the step that runs with everything it started and what the
steps of the job before it left behind and exits with 130, 143 or 129 and no
last line, "The end of a step" below.

What runs, and what does not. Every step with a run: block, in the order of the
file, job by job, except three kinds, each said in the output: a step with
uses: and no run: (an action of the platform, nothing to run here); a step
whose name is one of the two the order of 31 October 2026 left out, "what the
checkout holds", which prints the runner's own tools and checkout, and "what
the container needs, under Alpine alone", which installs bash, git and python3
into the Alpine container with apk; and a step carrying an if: field, since
this program evaluates no expression of the platform - such a step is not run
and counts as red, so that a step added under a condition shows up here until
this program learns its condition, the way the workflow's own hooks step goes
red on an event it has no input for. A job with a strategy.matrix runs on
GitHub once per entry of the matrix; here it runs once, on this system, and
the output says so.

The shell, from the file and from GitHub's docs. For every step the shell is
the step's own shell: field, else the job's defaults.run.shell, else the
workflow's defaults.run.shell, else none. GitHub's page "Workflow syntax for
GitHub Actions" (docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax,
read on 31 October 2026) says under jobs.<job_id>.steps[*].shell: "You can
override the default shell settings in the runner's operating system and the
job's default using the shell keyword", and gives for each shell the command
run internally - with no shell given, on Linux and macOS, bash -e {0}, "The
default shell on non-Windows platforms. Note that this runs a different
command to when bash is specified explicitly. If bash is not found in the
path, this is treated as sh."; for bash, bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail
{0}, "The default shell on non-Windows platforms with a fallback to sh. When
specifying a bash shell on Windows, the bash shell included with Git for
Windows is used."; for sh, sh -e {0}, "The fallback behavior for non-Windows
platforms if no shell is provided and bash is not found in the path."; for
pwsh, pwsh -command ". '{0}'", "This is the default shell used on Windows";
for python, python {0}. Under "Exit codes and error action preference": "By
default, fail-fast behavior is enforced using set -e for both sh and bash",
and "When shell: bash is specified, -o pipefail is also applied to enforce
early exit from pipelines." Under jobs.<job_id>.defaults.run: "Use
jobs.<job_id>.defaults.run to provide default shell and working-directory to
all run steps in the job", and under defaults.run, "a default setting defined
in a job will override a default setting that has the same name defined in a
workflow". So a run: block of the job stock-take, which sets no shell, ends
at the first command that fails and not at a failing command inside a
pipeline, and one of the job hooks on every system, whose defaults.run.shell
is bash, ends at either, the difference pull request #205 met on 25 October
2026. {0} is the path of a file holding the block: GitHub writes the block
into a temporary file and runs the command on it; here the file lies in a
directory mktemp makes outside the repository, removed at the end. A shell
this program does not run - cmd, powershell, or one it does not know - makes
the step red with a line saying so; a custom shell, a command carrying {0}, is
run as written.

The environment. A step gets the environment this program was started in,
and on top of it the env: maps of the workflow, the job and the step, the
step's winning, with ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }} replaced by the
head of the new entry the branch adds to docs/roadmap.md - the first line
git diff origin/main...HEAD -- docs/roadmap.md adds that starts with "- **",
read in the file to its closing "**", its lines joined with one space, the
text between the markers, as the workflow's step on titles reads a head -
and empty where the diff adds none, said in the output; any other
${{ ... }} in an env: value or in a run: block leaves the step unrun and red,
since nothing here stands in for the event. Three variables the runner sets
and the steps read are set here too: GITHUB_WORKSPACE, the root of this
repository, which is also every step's working directory unless a
working-directory names another below it; RUNNER_OS, Linux, macOS or Windows
for this system, as GitHub spells them; RUNNER_TEMP, the throwaway directory
above.

The end of a step. On macOS, Linux and Alpine every step runs under a holder,
since 16 November 2026: a process forked off this program that leads a
session of its own, starts the step's shell as its child and says over a pipe
of its own how the shell ended, so that the end of the step's own process is
read apart from the end of its output. A step that ends in time counts with
its own exit code, as GitHub counts it. This program reads the step's output
to its end and for no longer than five seconds after the shell has ended,
going on at once where the output closes sooner, and where a process the
step left behind holds the output still, a line under the output says so and
the step counts all the same; the five seconds are not held against the
job's timeout-minutes, the step itself having ended in time. GitHub's runner,
read in actions/runner at v2.338.2: where the step's process has exited and a
reader of its output is still open it waits five seconds,
Runner.Sdk/ProcessInvoker.cs lines 485 to 505 with the delay at line 494,
going on at once where the output closes, lines 523 to 527, ends nothing
after them under Linux and macOS, lines 855 to 869, the step's own process
having exited, and returns that process's exit code, line 377. This program
closes its end of the output then, so that a process left behind that writes
to it later is sent SIGPIPE; the runner disposes of the process object then,
lines 386 to 393, and what that does to its end of the output is not read.
Until that day the output was read to its end before the step was counted: a
step that had ended and left a process holding its output counted as stopped
at the job's timeout, its exit code lost, and without a timeout-minutes this
program waited for as long as the process held the output, measured on 15
November 2026 at 2107e12 on macOS, red after 3.07 seconds under a timeout of
0.05 minutes and not back after twelve seconds without one.

What a step leaves behind is ended at the end of its job, since the same day,
as GitHub ends it there. The holder stands on after the step, and when the
next job begins, when the last step is done or when this program is ended by
a signal, this program ends for every step of the job what the holder's
session, its process group or a parent ties to the step, the way it ends a
step at the job's timeout, below, and says in one line for every step that
had left something how many processes that were. Until then the process
lives: a later step of the job finds it, as on GitHub, and the next job does
not, as on GitHub, where every job has a runner of its own. GitHub's runner
ends at the job's end every process that carries the job's
RUNNER_TRACKING_ID in its environment, wherever it stands,
Runner.Worker/JobExtension.cs lines 588 to 603 and 891 to 939, the lines
"Terminate orphan process" of a job's log; this program ties by parent,
session and process group and not by the environment, below. The holder is
there for its number: this program does not wait for it until what the step
left is ended, and no other process is given a number while a process that
carries it stands or has ended and is not waited for, or while a session or
a process group carries it, bsd/kern/kern_fork.c lines 957 to 974 of
xnu-12377.121.6 and kernel/pid.c lines 393 to 413 with kernel/exit.c lines
134 to 147 of Linux at v7.0.14. So the number this program looks by at the
job's end is the step's still, however long the job ran, and the number of
the step's shell, which may be another process's by then, is read by
nothing. Until that day a step that ended in time and left a process behind
was not touched, and the process ran on once this program had returned,
measured on 15 November 2026 at 2107e12 on macOS with a step that starts
sleep 7401 > /dev/null 2>&1 & and ends.

A job's timeout-minutes stops a step that runs past it, red, as GitHub stops
the job, the minutes held against each step and not against the job as a
whole, and since 11 November 2026 the step is ended with everything it
started, so that nothing of it runs on once this program has returned. At the
timeout, and at the job's end for what a step left, this program stops before
it ends, since 16 November 2026: it sends SIGSTOP to the holder's process
group at once and then, listing after listing of ps -A -o pid,ppid,stat, to
every process that has not ended and whose parent, session or process group
is a process already known as the step's, until a listing shows none that
was not known, five seconds at most, the first listing taken before the
first signal; then it sends all of them SIGKILL and looks until a listing
shows none of them that is not a zombie, the same five seconds, sending
SIGKILL to what appears meanwhile in the holder's session or process group;
at the timeout it then reads the step's output to its end. A stopped process
neither ends nor starts another, so that its number stays its own until the
SIGKILL, and a process one level down in a session of its own, which hangs
on the step by its parent alone, still has that parent at the next listing.
From 11 November 2026 until that day SIGKILL went out listing after listing
and the numbers found were kept from one listing to the next: a number whose
process had ended and been waited for between two listings and had been
given to another process would have had the signal, and so would that
process's children, read and not measured, the block of milestone 12 in
docs/defects.md on the numbers kept between two listings. That reaches what
the step's shell started and waits for, what has lost its parent, what
stands in a process group of its own, as the command and the watchdog of
bin/devloop-bounded do under the hooks step, and what was started in a
session of its own, as this program starts a step one level down in its own
self-test. It does not reach a process that left the step's session and its
parent on purpose, a daemon, at the timeout or at the job's end: no listing
ties such a process to the step. GitHub's runner ends that one too, by the
variable in its environment; at the timeout it ends the step's one process,
after SIGINT and SIGTERM, ten seconds in all, which this program does not
send, Runner.Sdk/ProcessInvoker.cs lines 32 to 33, 443 to 465 and 855 to
869. The environment of another process is not to be read on every system
this program runs on. Under Linux /proc/PID/environ gives it, and on macOS
the kernel gives a process of the same user the environment of no program
of the system while System Integrity Protection is on: measured on 15
November 2026 on macOS 26.6.2, a sleep and a bash started with a mark in
their environment after a fork, setsid and a second fork showed none of
their environment to sysctl with KERN_PROCARGS2, to ps e -p PID -o command,
as the runner reads it, Runner.Common/ProcessExtensions.cs lines 319 to 354,
or to ps -E, where a python3 that is no program of the system showed the
mark to all three, and in alpine:3.22 /proc/PID/environ showed it for all
of them; the kernel leaves the environment out unless the process read is
the reader itself, is not restricted by its code signature, System
Integrity Protection is off or the reader carries an entitlement of
Apple's, bsd/kern/kern_sysctl.c lines 1480 to 1499 of xnu-12377.121.6, and
both programs of the system carried CS_RESTRICT. The block of milestone 12
in docs/defects.md on the process that left the step's session holds that
limit, and nothing is built for it. Where ps does not answer, the holder's
process group alone is ended, and where something still stands after the
five seconds, the line of the step says so behind "as GitHub stops the job"
at the timeout, as it does where something still holds the step's output
five seconds after that, and a line of its own says so at the job's end.
Until 11 November 2026 the step ran in this program's own process group and
at the timeout subprocess.run ended its one process, the shell, by SIGKILL:
what the shell had started ran on, measured on 11 November 2026 at 46030ab
on macOS, and under Linux by the measurement that came with the order of
that day, a sleep 30 with parent 1 standing a second after the self-test had
passed. A step in a session of its own is reached by no signal sent to this
program's process group, Ctrl-C at a terminal and the SIGTERM of
bin/devloop-bounded among them, so that since the same day this program takes
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP in hand: ended by one of them, it ends the step
that runs the same way and, since 16 November 2026, what the steps of the
job before it left behind, ignores the three from the first of them on, so
that no second signal cuts that short, removes its directory and exits with
128 and the signal's number; a signal that was ignored when this program
started stays ignored, as SIGINT is where a shell without job control
started it in the background, and the step inherits that; ended by SIGKILL
it leaves the step running and what steps left behind, the holders of the
steps that have ended ending by themselves once this program is gone. A
step that ends the holder it runs under before its own shell has ended is
red with a line that says so, and is ended with everything it started.
Under Windows none of this is built and a step runs as before: no holder,
at the timeout its one process is ended and nothing it started, the program
then waits for the step's output to close, a step that has ended is counted
once its output has closed, and nothing a step left behind is looked for;
the block of milestone 12 in docs/defects.md on Windows holds that.

The file is read by this program's own reader of the YAML the workflows of
this repository are written in: mappings and sequences by indentation, a
sequence item opening a mapping on its own line, literal and folded block
scalars with their chomping, quoted and plain scalars, flow sequences in
brackets, comments outside block scalars; nothing else is needed for
.github/workflows/stock-take.yml, and a form it does not read ends with 2
and the line. It needs python3 and git, and on macOS, Linux and Alpine ps, at
the end of every job that ran a step, where a step runs past the timeout and
where the program is ended, and no GNU tool. The steps it runs need what they
need on GitHub, python3 and git, and the self-test needs bash, sh and, on
macOS, Linux and Alpine, pgrep on PATH.

The self-test writes a workflow of its own and a throwaway repository with a
new entry head on a branch, runs this program over it, and reads the outcomes:
the default shell without pipefail against shell: bash with it on the same
block, set -e ending a block at the first failing command, a step's own shell
over the job's default, PR_TITLE out of the event, a block with a heredoc and
blank lines, the three kinds of step not run, a shell this program cannot
run, a job stopped at its timeout, a matrix job run once, --list running
nothing, exit 1 and 0 on a red and a green file, exit 2 on a file that is no
workflow; on macOS, Linux and Alpine, since 11 November 2026, the step past the
timeout ended with everything it started, a sleep that has lost its parent,
one in a process group of its own and the one its shell waits for, sleep 7361,
7363 and 7365, none of them found by pgrep -f once the program has returned,
under a pattern bound at the start of the command line, which a zombie, having
no command line, does not match, the program ended by SIGTERM while a step
runs, exit 143 and the step's sleeps 7367 and 7369 gone, and a step past the
timeout that runs this program one level down, the sleep 7371 of that one's
step gone as well; since 16 November 2026, on the same three systems, the
program ended by SIGTERM in the second step of a workflow whose first step
has left sleep 7395 behind, that one gone as well; a job of four steps under
a timeout of 0.05 minutes, a step that leaves sleep 7381 holding nothing of
its output and counts at once, the step after it finding that sleep, a step
that ends with 3 and leaves sleep 7383 behind a second in which its output
is held, counted with 3 and nothing said of its output, and a step that ends
with 0 and leaves sleep 7385 in a process group of its own holding its
output, counted with 0 after the five seconds with the line that says so and
not stopped at the timeout, at the job's end a line for each of the three,
a second job that finds none of them, and none found by pgrep once the
program has returned; ps not answering at the job's end, a stand-in first on
